  "query_preview": "..Clear skies this morning across southeast Arizona and \nits shaping up to be another pleasant day with slightly below normal \ntemperatures as some high clouds passing through with westerly flow \naloft.\n\nThe next weather system is now located over the Pacific NW and is \ndigging towards the southeast today towards the Great Basin. For the \npast couple of days we've been talking about the potential to become \na closed low and dig farther south and west into AZ compared to a \npotentially more progressive open wave solution. Latest trends \ncontinue towards the more closed low solution with the ECMWF/EPS \nleading the way and the other model solutions seemingly playing \ncatch up. With that said, this upper low will push south across west-\ncentral AZ Friday and towards southern AZ Friday evening with \nlocales east of the track of the upper low having the potential for \nscattered shower activity (20 to 50 percent chances) with a slight \nchance of thunderstorms starting Friday afternoon from Tucson area \neastward, with shower chances lingering east of Tucson Friday night \ninto Saturday. This isn't looking like a very impactful event with \nQPF values mainly less than 0.25\". \n\nOnce any lingering shower chances depart to our east on Saturday, \ndry and seasonable conditions will then prevail through the \nremainder of the forecast period. As surface high pressure builds \ndown the front range of the Rockies this weekend, easterly flow will \nprevail in the lower levels for some breezes, especially for locales \nmost susceptible for east winds. The next more significant weather \nsystem could impact our region around next Friday into that \nfollowing weekend.",
